v5.2.2
Radon Player 5.2.2: Copy songs between your devices, and widgets that work everywhere
**Install or update:** `RadonPlayer-Next.apk` (Android), `Radon-Player-5.2.2.msi` (Windows), `radon-player_5.2.2_amd64.deb` (Linux). Each installs over the version you have and keeps your settings and stats.

**What is new**
- **New releases and Visualizer widgets on the phone:** **New releases** lists the newest releases the app has found from your artists (cover, title, artist, how long ago); a tap plays one without opening the app, and the name opens New releases. **Visualizer** shows the music as bars in the cover's colours with the song, play/pause and next; it only moves while the screen is on, the app isn't in front and music plays (about 8 frames a second), and rests otherwise. Moving bars need the microphone permission the app's Visualizer already asks for; without it they sway slowly.
- **New releases and Visualizer widgets on the PC:** **New releases** (a click plays one, the name opens New releases) and **Visualizer** (your visualizer style in the song's colours, with play/pause and next when the mouse is over it; it rests while paused), from the tray icon's Widgets or Settings › General. **Make desktop shortcuts…** can also make a New releases shortcut.
- **Copy songs between your phone and PC (the phone and the PC):** in Settings › Remote & sync › **Other device's songs**, a song can now come as its own file from your other device, over the same connection Sync uses (Wi-Fi or Tailscale, no USB): your unreleased songs and rare versions arrive exactly as they are, with their tags, cover and lyrics. **Copy the file from your other device** picks **Always when it has the file**, **Only when it can't be found on a streaming service** (at first) or **Never**, and each song says which way it comes (↓ Tidal or ⇄ from your PC). A dropped connection carries on where it stopped, and each file is checked before it's saved. Both apps need to be open, as for Sync now; a song the PC asks for is sent by the phone the next time it checks in.
- **PC widgets that show up and don't slow anything (Linux):** on Linux the widgets never appeared and slowed the whole app down (they were see-through windows the graphics card couldn't show). They're now plain cards the desktop rounds itself, they open where you left them and at their size (on Hyprland too), and the app stays smooth. **Always on top** is off unless you turn it on, so other windows go over the widgets.
- **Shortcuts with plain names and their own icons (the PC):** "Wrapped all time", "DJ with only my songs" or the playlist's name, each with an icon that shows what it does (a playlist's own cover). A new **Shortcuts** widget puts the ones you made on your screen as one-click buttons, for desktops without desktop icons (Hyprland and the like), where the app no longer puts files that only opened a text editor. **Add to the taskbar** (a pin next to each shortcut) puts it on Omarchy's taskbar or GNOME's dash after your own pins; on Windows, **Open the shortcuts folder** and pin one by hand (right-click › Pin to taskbar).
- **Phone widgets polished:** they keep up with the music even when the app was started from a widget or headphones without its window, keep rounded corners in the cover's colour on every Android version, stay readable on bright covers, never cut text off at their smallest sizes, and Play, Next and Previous start the music without opening the app. The queue on Now playing stays right after you reorder it, and while the phone is the PC's remote the widgets show the PC's song. The Visualizer only moves while the phone is unlocked (and rests after ten minutes); Most played no longer wakes the phone every hour.
